hi there Steve Kaufman here again and  today I want to talk about multitasking  and language learning but first if you  like these videos please subscribe to my  channel now I mentioned in my previous  video that I wanted to increase my  ability to notice things in Ukranian and  that therefore I was gonna be saving  phrases almost every phrase that contain  for example case endings a because just  even saving them helps me notice and B  because I can then review them in our  vocabulary section you know isolated by  a particular case so then I said well I  should try this in other languages so I  decided to choose Italian and in our  feed section of link we have we can see  articles that other people are studying  we can see newspaper articles which we  can import in the link so I chose one on  multitasking and I went through saving  phrases with tenses which sometimes  cause trouble in Italian I only need to  save that tense like tag it for a tense  I don't need a tag it for first second  or third person singular or plural  because if I capture the whole phrase  it'll be obvious who or what the subject  of that verb is so I was doing that but  of course the article was very  interesting and one of the delights of  language learning as you get past the  beginner stage is that you can actually  improve your language by reading or  listening to things that are interesting  so this article said multitasking is bad  it's bad for the brain and so I started  to think well what do they mean by  multitasking obviously if you're working  in an office and you're trying to do two  things at once you're going to be  distracted and probably that's bad for  the brain however when it comes to  language learning if I couldn't  multitask I don't think I would learn my  languages or it would take me much much  longer because probably 75% of my  learning activity is multitasking  primarily listening  I listen while exercising while driving  while doing the dishes and I'm almost  better able to concentrate while doing  some other relatively repetitive or call  it non thinking type tasks while  listening I may sometimes you know focus  in focus out but by and large I'm able  to get in 45 minutes a day an hour a day  of listening I look forward to doing the  dishes so that I can listen to my  language I then may concentrate sit down  and read and save phrases and work these  texts so that if I listen again I will  understand more and I will notice more  but it's very essential that I have this  ability to multitask and I think people  should develop that skill I sometimes  hear people say well I I can't focus  while I'm listening well maybe that's a  skill that you have to develop I can  even read on my iPad while I'm on my  treadmill and not a treadmill but I have  a stepper at home but I've seen people  on treadmills in health clubs who watch  TV programs while they're jogging so  there are tasks that you can do in  combination with other more or less  repetitive tasks and for language  learning I would say that developing the  ability to multitask at least the  ability to listen and to focus 60 70 80  percent of the time while listening and  doing other relatively repetitive tasks  is an important skill and even the  ability to read while say exercising the  reading is more difficult obviously you  can't read and do the dishes you can't  read and drive but you can read and be  on a treadmill so that was just  something that I wanted to mention and  and also I wanted to close by saying  that this issue of saving phrases of  link I think it's extremely important  and perhaps that we don't emphasize  strongly enough just how crucial it is  because you learn so much  words it's very difficult to cover all  of these words in your flash guarding or  other review type activities but the  number of phrases you need in order to  capture the essential structures of a  language is much much smaller so that if  you can focus on phrases save those  phrases tag those phrases review those  phrases in the vocabulary section you're  going to get a real good handle on the  structure of the language and pick up a  lot of vocabulary along the way so I  might just do a separate video in the  future about how I actually do this at  any rate multitasking I believe in so  far as language is concerned is  important and it's a skill that we need  to develop if we're going to be  efficient language learners thank you  for listening bye for now  [Music]
 

